  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1244 AM CDT Fri May 22 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough over the southwestern US will move into the \nsouthern Plains during the day on Saturday.  Meanwhile a surface \nfront will drop southward into northwest Texas on Saturday morning. \nAhead of the front, a warm, moist airmass will continue to be in \nplace across west central Texas with dewpoints in the 60s. As a \nresult, thunderstorm chances should return for Saturday along and \nahead of this front, mainly in the afternoon. Severe chances look \nconfined to our far southeast counties because this is where the \nbest upper-level support will be. Regardless, heavy rain and \nflooding concerns look to continue for our eastern counties where \nPWAT values over 1.5 inches will be prevalent.\n\nThe axis of the upper trough should drift into east Texas on Sunday \nwhere it will become a bit more defined before becoming a closed low \non Monday.  This progression would place west central Texas on the \nbackside of the low which is less favorable for precipitation \noverall. Given the persistently humid airmass, we'll keep low \nchances for rain in our far eastern counties for Sunday.  The \nMemorial Day holiday, likewise, looks dry with low rain chances and \nhighs in the 80s.  \n\nLooking ahead toward the middle of next week, models are in fairly \ngood agreement on digging a deeper trough southward from the Pacific \nNorthwest through the western US.  A smaller, negatively-tilted \ntrough would be pushed out ahead of it into west Texas by Wednesday. \nThis would bring another round of potentially active weather through \nwest central Texas with high temperatures near to slightly below \nnormal.",
